[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
OSPF is healthy; no regional outage is present. On R1, the OSPF RIB still contains all R7-originated routes (e.g., 67.0.0.0/8, 70.0.0.0/8, and multiple 17.1.x.1/32 host routes), proving reachability to the R7 region is intact. On the R7 side, OSPF adjacency is up: r7 shows a Full neighbor with R6 on 67.1.1.0/24, and r6 shows a Full neighbor with R7 (router-id 67.1.1.2). R7 is running the FRR daemons (watchfrr, ospfd, zebra) as confirmed by process listing, and its OSPF routes are actively installed. The “frr.service” systemd unit on R7 shows “inactive (dead)”, but this is cosmetic: the daemons are started by watchfrr directly, not via the frr.service unit in this lab container, which can confuse process-status-based monitoring.

Mechanism: the monitoring likely keys off the systemd unit state or a stale snapshot of R1’s route view, so when the unit reads “inactive,” it falsely infers OSPF is down and all R7 routes vanished. Localization: monitoring/alerting logic rather than any router. Root cause: misconfigured monitoring relying on the R7 frr.service unit status (inactive) instead of validating OSPF adjacency or RIB/LSDB health. Corrective fix: change monitors to check ospfd process existence or OSPF neighbor state (e.g., r6<->r7 Full) and/or verify R1’s RIB for 67/8, 70/8, and 17.x routes; optionally enable and manage FRR via the systemd frr.service to align with monitoring. No restart required. 
=== END ===